Implemented Vercel Web Analytics for Next.js App Router project

## Changes Made:

### 1. Installed @vercel/analytics package
- Added `@vercel/analytics` to project dependencies using npm
- Package version installed based on latest stable release

### 2. Modified app/layout.jsx
- Added import statement: `import { Analytics } from "@vercel/analytics/next";`
- Placed `<Analytics />` component at the end of the body section
- Followed official Vercel documentation for Next.js App Router integration
- Placement after Footer component ensures analytics tracking is loaded after main content

### 3. Updated Lock Files
- Updated package-lock.json with new dependency tree

## Implementation Details:

The implementation follows the official Vercel Analytics quickstart guide (fetched from https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart) for Next.js App Router projects.

The Analytics component was placed inside the `<body>` tag at the end, which is the recommended position according to Vercel's documentation. This ensures:
- The component loads after all main content
- Minimal impact on initial page load performance
- Proper tracking of page views and web vitals

## Files Modified:
- `app/layout.jsx` - Added Analytics import and component
- `package.json` - Added @vercel/analytics dependency
- `package-lock.json` - Updated with new dependency tree

## Verification:
- Build completed successfully without errors
- All static pages generated correctly (7/7 pages)
- No TypeScript or linting errors introduced
- Build output shows proper JS bundle sizes

## Next Steps:
After deployment to Vercel:
1. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard (Analytics section)
2. Deploy the application to production
3. Analytics data will begin appearing in the dashboard after user visits accumulate
